00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:06.830
But I just want
to go over briefly

00:02:06.830 --> 00:02:09.919
what our corporate
architecture looks like.

00:02:09.919 --> 00:02:13.440
It's probably very similar
to a lot of companies.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:15.260
So we have users.

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:16.850
They have browsers.

00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:21.860
They talk to a lot of hosted
SaaS apps, Slack, G Suite,

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:25.730
GitHub, tons of these apps.

00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:30.620
But we also have a large
corporate infrastructure

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:32.700
of what we run ourselves.

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:39.860
So this is composed of
applications primarily running

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:43.180
on AWS, along with
some applications that

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:45.560
run in some local computer.

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:49.760
All of Airbnb.com runs on AWS.

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:51.530
But we have some
supporting services

00:02:51.530 --> 00:02:54.560
that run with some
local compute, primarily

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:58.370
for some latency
reasons with SIP routing

00:02:58.370 --> 00:03:00.030
and things like that.

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:03.250
But this is really what our
infrastructure looks like,

00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:06.800
scaled down,
simplified, very basic.

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:10.790
But we've invested heavily
in end point instrumentation

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:11.910
and reporting.

00:03:11.910 --> 00:03:14.280
We use tools like
Osquery from Facebook,

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:16.610
along with a tool that
we developed internally

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:19.730
called StreamAlert,
which runs in AWS Lambda

00:03:19.730 --> 00:03:23.650
and analyzes the results
coming from Osquery suite.

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:25.670
We have a ton of
data that we look

00:03:25.670 --> 00:03:30.560
at coming from our end points.

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:35.420
And then our users get to our
internal services through VPN

00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:39.750
or 802.1X when they're
in an Airbnb office,

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:42.380
whether that's on a wired
connection or a wireless

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:45.090
connection.

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:48.580
If you're not in office, then
you're going to be using VPN.

00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:51.350
So this is basically what our
infrastructure looks like.

00:03:55.070 --> 00:03:56.810
There's a very strange
transition there.

00:03:56.810 --> 00:04:05.360
So Airbnb was founded just a few
blocks away from here in SoMa

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:06.770
about 10 years ago.

00:04:06.770 --> 00:04:14.570
And over the past
decade, has grown out

00:04:14.570 --> 00:04:16.310
of just San Francisco.

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:19.370
And we now have offices
all around the world,

00:04:19.370 --> 00:04:21.769
with thousands of
users supporting

00:04:21.769 --> 00:04:24.990
our community of millions
of hosts and guests.

00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:28.460
Our largest office is still
here in San Francisco,

00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:30.500
but with all of
these offices, we

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:35.120
ended up building a
network for the users

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:38.550
to get to our services.

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:42.080
But this growth has presented
us with a constant challenge.

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:45.530
When you're just one
office, it's pretty easy

00:04:45.530 --> 00:04:49.330
to get into your resources
that might be running

00:04:49.330 --> 00:04:55.940
in AWS by whitelisting the IP
of your network or something

00:04:55.940 --> 00:04:58.500
like that.

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:04.200
But as we've expanded,
that hasn't really worked.

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:06.320
So what we've
realized with that is

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:09.730
that the worst experiences
that our users have

00:05:09.730 --> 00:05:11.930
are inconsistent ones.

00:05:11.930 --> 00:05:15.820
So while you might
be in San Francisco

00:05:15.820 --> 00:05:19.990
and be able to get everything,
if you go to Portland,

00:05:19.990 --> 00:05:21.620
you haven't gone very far.

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:24.880
That's still a
large Airbnb office.

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:32.440
But you might not be able to
access everything that we have.

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:35.410
And the problem really just
gets worse the farther away

00:05:35.410 --> 00:05:38.700
that you get from San Francisco.

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:40.910
Or it's gotten worse.

00:05:40.910 --> 00:05:45.210
So a couple of years
ago, we started

00:05:45.210 --> 00:05:47.710
a project called Work Anywhere.

00:05:47.710 --> 00:05:51.030
And this comes out of
three kind of realizations.

00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:55.420
The first is that securing
user networks is pretty hard,

00:05:55.420 --> 00:05:57.120
at least doing it
in a way that's

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:00.130
user friendly and scalable.

00:06:00.130 --> 00:06:04.680
So for us, 802.1X and VPN have
had a lot of edge cases where

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:05.790
they don't really work.

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:13.830
So in the case of 802.1X, when
we're expanding into a new

00:06:13.830 --> 00:06:18.030
city, we might start off
in a managed service office

00:06:18.030 --> 00:06:20.550
or something like that, where
we don't control the whole

00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:21.450
network.

00:06:21.450 --> 00:06:24.960
So it'll look like
an Airbnb office.

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:26.360
It'll feel like one.

00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:30.030
But the network that they're
coming through isn't run by us.

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:32.350
It's really just an
internet connection.

00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:36.720
So there are generally ways
that we could end up deploying

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:40.590
802.1X there through remote
access points or something like

00:06:40.590 --> 00:06:41.680
that.

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:45.030
But generally,
getting those offices,

00:06:45.030 --> 00:06:50.110
which are Airbnb offices, on
the network is not so easy.

00:06:50.110 --> 00:06:54.690
So we end up having
those users who use VPN.

00:06:54.690 --> 00:06:57.030
And then VPN has so
many edge cases, too,

00:06:57.030 --> 00:07:00.750
where VPN falls over--

00:07:00.750 --> 00:07:05.520
from users that are really far
away, from their VPN gateways,

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:10.260
or for users who are
flying in an airplane,

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:13.740
and they can't approve MFA,
or it's really hard for them

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:16.530
to get MFA working
when they're on VPN.

00:07:16.530 --> 00:07:21.570
There's just so many edge cases,
where just securing the network

00:07:21.570 --> 00:07:23.820
is hard.

00:07:23.820 --> 00:07:25.800
Then what we've
realized, that is

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:29.700
latency makes things even worse.

00:07:29.700 --> 00:07:33.040
We haven't been able to
defy physics quite yet.

00:07:33.040 --> 00:07:36.910
So when we have these
offices all over the world,

00:07:36.910 --> 00:07:40.740
and most of our applications
are running in US East

00:07:40.740 --> 00:07:46.200
in AWS, as most companies
end up running things

00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:51.510
in a single region
or a single country,

00:07:51.510 --> 00:07:56.080
the latency to these apps
can just be really bad.

00:07:56.080 --> 00:08:01.090
And we can't get you any
closer to that application.

00:08:01.090 --> 00:08:03.550
But it's really easy
to make things worse.

00:08:03.550 --> 00:08:06.460
So we've known about this
issue for a long time,

00:08:06.460 --> 00:08:12.180
particularly for users
who are in Australia.

00:08:12.180 --> 00:08:16.860
And if you are in Australia,
and say, you're in Sydney,

00:08:16.860 --> 00:08:19.007
and you want to
connect to a VPN,

00:08:19.007 --> 00:08:21.090
you're probably going to
connect to a VPN endpoint

00:08:21.090 --> 00:08:23.190
that we have running in
Singapore because the one

00:08:23.190 --> 00:08:24.893
that's closest to you.

00:08:24.893 --> 00:08:26.310
But if you're in
Sydney and you're

00:08:26.310 --> 00:08:28.920
connected to a VPN
gateway in Singapore,

00:08:28.920 --> 00:08:32.190
that traffic is going
to route actually

00:08:32.190 --> 00:08:35.220
way out of the way
to go to Singapore

00:08:35.220 --> 00:08:39.570
and then come all the way back
to the east coast of the US.

00:08:39.570 --> 00:08:43.110
It's over 50% farther
to go from Sydney

00:08:43.110 --> 00:08:45.990
to Singapore all
the way to the US

00:08:45.990 --> 00:08:49.440
than it is just to go
from Sydney to the US.

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:54.250
It might not be evident
without a globe,

00:08:54.250 --> 00:08:56.380
but that's the reality.

00:08:56.380 --> 00:09:00.570
And so latency becomes a
problem for even more users.

00:09:00.570 --> 00:09:05.430
It becomes really obvious for
me when I'm on an airplane,

00:09:05.430 --> 00:09:13.410
with the explosion of use of
satellite-based Wi-Fi on planes

00:09:13.410 --> 00:09:16.890
that's going to outer space.

00:09:16.890 --> 00:09:19.200
The best that you're
ever going to do there

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:22.470
is like 600 milliseconds.

00:09:22.470 --> 00:09:28.230
But if you are on a flight
from Tokyo to Los Angeles,

00:09:28.230 --> 00:09:30.180
your internet traffic
might actually

00:09:30.180 --> 00:09:33.960
be bouncing off a few satellites
and coming out in Europe.

00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:39.120
Now if you're on that flight
and you need to connect to VPN,

00:09:39.120 --> 00:09:40.605
which gateway do you pick?

00:09:40.605 --> 00:09:43.258
I mean, I would probably
pick the US one,

00:09:43.258 --> 00:09:45.300
but that's actually the
wrong one for me to pick.

00:09:45.300 --> 00:09:47.255
I should be connected
to the EU one.

00:09:47.255 --> 00:09:48.630
And there's a lot
of VPN software

00:09:48.630 --> 00:09:50.880
that can attempt to
make the right decision,

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:53.100
but we've just
realized that latency

00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:57.270
gets bad for a lot of
users all the time.

00:09:57.270 --> 00:09:59.800
And it's hard for users
to pick the right thing.

00:09:59.800 --> 00:10:05.100
And then lastly is that users
expect to work anywhere.

00:10:05.100 --> 00:10:06.870
If a user has a good
internet connection,

00:10:06.870 --> 00:10:10.590
they know that they have a
good internet connection.

00:10:10.590 --> 00:10:11.520
They can get to Gmail.

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:13.340
They can get to all these apps.

00:10:13.340 --> 00:10:18.240
But their connectivity
to our internal apps

00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:20.693
may not be so great.

00:10:20.693 --> 00:10:22.110
If they're far
away from a gateway

00:10:22.110 --> 00:10:24.780
or they're choosing
other ones, it's just not

00:10:24.780 --> 00:10:25.960
working great for them.

00:10:25.960 --> 00:10:28.440
So while a user
can theoretically

00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:30.370
work from anywhere
today, they're

00:10:30.370 --> 00:10:32.670
able to connect
to anything today.

00:10:32.670 --> 00:10:36.820
If they're over VPN, they
can't really work anywhere

00:10:36.820 --> 00:10:38.650
if it's a bad experience.

00:10:38.650 --> 00:10:43.100
So we just believe that if
a user has a good internet

00:10:43.100 --> 00:10:46.340
connection and a secure device,
they should be able to work.

00:10:46.340 --> 00:10:49.600
So that's where our Work
Anywhere project came from.

00:10:49.600 --> 00:10:52.530
And I think that's it from me.

00:10:52.530 --> 00:10:54.920
Oh, one more slide.

00:10:54.920 --> 00:10:57.420
So IT security at
Airbnb, so where

00:10:57.420 --> 00:11:00.360
does security come into this?

00:11:00.360 --> 00:11:02.050
So I'm just a security engineer.

00:11:02.050 --> 00:11:06.450
I've been mostly talking about
networking and user-facing

00:11:06.450 --> 00:11:07.480
stuff.

00:11:07.480 --> 00:11:10.980
And this slide could have
a lot of things on it,

00:11:10.980 --> 00:11:13.890
talking about what IT
security does at Airbnb.

00:11:13.890 --> 00:11:19.830
But I think it's just really
exemplified in this one

00:11:19.830 --> 00:11:21.840
photo of a security key.

00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:27.540
So what IT security
tries to do at Airbnb

00:11:27.540 --> 00:11:31.830
is find the best mix of
user experience and security

00:11:31.830 --> 00:11:33.970
to propel both forward.

00:11:33.970 --> 00:11:37.440
And I think that the
security key is really--

00:11:40.540 --> 00:11:44.950
it's the holy grail for
what that should be.

00:11:44.950 --> 00:11:48.190
So I'm proud to say
that we've deployed

00:11:48.190 --> 00:11:52.150
over 10,000 of these
security keys at Airbnb.

00:11:52.150 --> 00:11:57.280
And they the majority of
our multi-factor auths

00:11:57.280 --> 00:12:03.010
are happening with these
for our internal users.

00:12:03.010 --> 00:12:07.120
We no longer have any phone
call or SMS-based MFA,

00:12:07.120 --> 00:12:09.280
which are obviously insecure.

00:12:09.280 --> 00:12:11.660
But they were really just
a bad user experience.

00:12:11.660 --> 00:12:16.270
So we've been able to
take MFA from being

00:12:16.270 --> 00:12:20.050
a burden to something that
users have actually embraced

00:12:20.050 --> 00:12:21.760
and enjoy to use.

00:12:24.490 --> 00:12:27.460
And that's what
we've been trying

00:12:27.460 --> 00:12:31.600
to do with Work Anywhere, is
propel both user experience

00:12:31.600 --> 00:12:35.200
and security forward from
what we've had in the past.

00:12:35.200 --> 00:12:40.030
Because there's a lot of ways
that you can have a great user

00:12:40.030 --> 00:12:40.840
experience.

00:12:40.840 --> 00:12:42.843
If we just wanted the
best user experience,

00:12:42.843 --> 00:12:45.010
we just put all these apps
straight on the internet.

00:12:45.010 --> 00:12:48.170
But that would obviously
be horrible for security.

00:12:48.170 --> 00:12:50.210
So we've tried to
avoid to do that.

00:12:50.210 --> 00:12:52.210
So with that, I'm going
to hand it off to Ameet.

00:12:54.886 --> 00:12:55.969
AMEET JANI: Whoops, sorry.

00:12:55.969 --> 00:12:56.960
Thank you.

00:12:56.960 --> 00:12:58.622
He's coming back in a minute.

00:12:58.622 --> 00:13:00.080
He'll talk about
what they actually

00:13:00.080 --> 00:13:01.247
did to solve their problems.

00:13:01.247 --> 00:13:03.950
But I did want to just
transition here for a moment.

00:13:03.950 --> 00:13:06.320
All the things that
Samuel talked about

00:13:06.320 --> 00:13:08.600
with security problems and
all those different things,

00:13:08.600 --> 00:13:10.843
Google themselves,
we ourselves faced.

00:13:10.843 --> 00:13:12.260
About eight to 10
years ago, there

00:13:12.260 --> 00:13:15.470
was a well publicized thing of
state-sponsored hackers trying

00:13:15.470 --> 00:13:17.160
to get into our network.

00:13:17.160 --> 00:13:20.000
And so out of that, we
kind of changed our model

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:22.790
from a network-centric VPN model
to what we call the BeyondCorp

00:13:22.790 --> 00:13:24.800
or zero trust model.

00:13:24.800 --> 00:13:27.480
And so really, there's
three tenets of this model.

00:13:27.480 --> 00:13:30.077
The first one is that you no
longer care about the network

00:13:30.077 --> 00:13:31.160
that somebody's coming on.

00:13:31.160 --> 00:13:33.660
You really, really care about
the context of that request.

00:13:33.660 --> 00:13:35.535
And I'm not talking
about five or six things.

00:13:35.535 --> 00:13:37.580
I'm talking about
120 different things.

00:13:37.580 --> 00:13:40.520
And you're really thinking
about that on every request

00:13:40.520 --> 00:13:41.350
to an application.

00:13:41.350 --> 00:13:43.070
And that has a lot of
ramifications about how

00:13:43.070 --> 00:13:44.320
you deploy these applications.

00:13:44.320 --> 00:13:45.775
And we'll talk about that.

00:13:45.775 --> 00:13:47.150
And the key is
that you should be

00:13:47.150 --> 00:13:49.527
able to create granular
level access controls based

00:13:49.527 --> 00:13:51.860
on if somebody's coming from
a corporate device or maybe

00:13:51.860 --> 00:13:54.278
a known or trusted network
versus a non-corporate device.

00:13:54.278 --> 00:13:56.570
Those people shouldn't be
shut out of being productive.

00:13:56.570 --> 00:13:58.670
It just means that what they
get access to should be limited.

00:13:58.670 --> 00:14:00.212
And we'll talk about
how we did that.

00:14:00.212 --> 00:14:03.200
But this is exactly the model
that Google started to follow.

00:14:03.200 --> 00:14:05.222
And so when we were putting
this model together,

00:14:05.222 --> 00:14:06.680
we kind of hit on
a few key tenets.

00:14:06.680 --> 00:14:08.340
I don't think there's anything
that's rocket science here,

00:14:08.340 --> 00:14:10.757
but it's really important to
just walk through and kind of

00:14:10.757 --> 00:14:12.180
understand all the components.

00:14:12.180 --> 00:14:14.360
We can talk about all the
solutions for everybody.

00:14:14.360 --> 00:14:17.120
The first one that we discovered
is very, very important

00:14:17.120 --> 00:14:18.200
to understand the users.

00:14:18.200 --> 00:14:21.135
I think that's obvious, but
there's a secondary portion

00:14:21.135 --> 00:14:22.760
of that thing, which
is that you wanted

00:14:22.760 --> 00:14:24.650
to understand user behavior.

00:14:24.650 --> 00:14:26.880
If you log in from your
mother's house every Tuesday,

00:14:26.880 --> 00:14:28.880
we shouldn't care that
it's your mother's house,

00:14:28.880 --> 00:14:31.087
but we do want to know that
you have these patterns

00:14:31.087 --> 00:14:31.670
and behaviors.

00:14:31.670 --> 00:14:33.630
And if something starts
to fall out of the norm,

00:14:33.630 --> 00:14:35.422
we can start to at
least create speed bumps

00:14:35.422 --> 00:14:36.680
for users along the way.

00:14:36.680 --> 00:14:38.388
The second part of
this is second factor.

00:14:38.388 --> 00:14:40.455
And we'll touch on
that in a minute.

00:14:40.455 --> 00:14:42.830
The other part of that whole
side of things that we found

00:14:42.830 --> 00:14:45.350
is even if you trust a user,
you can attest very strongly

00:14:45.350 --> 00:14:49.080
that the users who they say they
are, the device posture really,

00:14:49.080 --> 00:14:49.920
really matters.

00:14:49.920 --> 00:14:51.080
And I'll tell you
personally at Google,

00:14:51.080 --> 00:14:52.790
I think we published
this in a white paper.

00:14:52.790 --> 00:14:53.707
We have the team here.

00:14:53.707 --> 00:14:54.500
You can ask them.

00:14:54.500 --> 00:14:56.480
Something like 125
characteristics

00:14:56.480 --> 00:14:59.570
about a request from
the device itself.

00:14:59.570 --> 00:15:01.680
That's the two major components.

00:15:01.680 --> 00:15:04.010
But then there's a whole
other context portion of this

00:15:04.010 --> 00:15:05.135
that we really cared about.

00:15:05.135 --> 00:15:07.100
And so we run a global
front end at Google,

00:15:07.100 --> 00:15:09.620
which captures which IP address
you're running coming from.

00:15:09.620 --> 00:15:11.870
We happen to know, we happen
to be constantly scanning

00:15:11.870 --> 00:15:14.360
the internet to understand what
are good IP addresses, what

00:15:14.360 --> 00:15:17.507
are VPN IP addresses, what are
state-sponsored IP addresses.

00:15:17.507 --> 00:15:19.340
And we can start to
block access from those.

00:15:19.340 --> 00:15:21.923
We also understand location, and
time of day, and session age.

00:15:21.923 --> 00:15:24.380
All this stuff needs to be
collected at the request point

00:15:24.380 --> 00:15:26.090
every single time.

00:15:26.090 --> 00:15:27.590
But then the real
smarts for us came

00:15:27.590 --> 00:15:29.792
in when we started
coming down the chain.

00:15:29.792 --> 00:15:31.250
The rules engine
portion, we needed

00:15:31.250 --> 00:15:32.990
to be able to create
human readable

00:15:32.990 --> 00:15:36.110
and very easy to define rules
that took all those context

00:15:36.110 --> 00:15:38.140
and took into account.

00:15:38.140 --> 00:15:40.695
So you can apply them at, in
fact, an end point, right?

00:15:40.695 --> 00:15:42.320
And so for us that
we wanted to protect

00:15:42.320 --> 00:15:44.835
every piece of data
that's on the web for us,

00:15:44.835 --> 00:15:45.710
we needed to protect.

00:15:45.710 --> 00:15:47.150
That's not just HTTPS.

00:15:47.150 --> 00:15:49.737
It is every type of piece
of data that's out there.

00:15:49.737 --> 00:15:51.320
And so that was sort
of how we came up

00:15:51.320 --> 00:15:53.070
with our BeyondCorp model.

00:15:53.070 --> 00:15:55.340
And we put tooling in
place that revolves

00:15:55.340 --> 00:15:57.205
around all of this stuff.

00:15:57.205 --> 00:15:59.330
And then what happened
about two or three years ago

00:15:59.330 --> 00:16:00.788
is we decided to
start productizing

00:16:00.788 --> 00:16:01.770
these piece by piece.

00:16:01.770 --> 00:16:04.160
And so what you're seeing
here is that really, the story

00:16:04.160 --> 00:16:06.740
is more of a suite of solutions
than it is one singular

00:16:06.740 --> 00:16:07.557
solution.

00:16:07.557 --> 00:16:09.140
And I'll touch on
all of these things.

00:16:09.140 --> 00:16:11.640
And the good news is you don't
need to adopt all or nothing.

00:16:11.640 --> 00:16:13.490
There is a gradual
crawl, walk, run model

00:16:13.490 --> 00:16:16.830
that we find ourselves at Google
using, we find customers using.

00:16:16.830 --> 00:16:18.860
And so we can talk about
how that would work.

00:16:18.860 --> 00:16:20.900
But just touching on
each for a moment here,

00:16:20.900 --> 00:16:23.357
let's focus-- just turn
to these very quickly.

00:16:23.357 --> 00:16:24.690
The first one is Cloud Identity.

00:16:24.690 --> 00:16:26.315
Each one of you has
an identity system.

00:16:26.315 --> 00:16:27.470
You don't need to use ours.

00:16:27.470 --> 00:16:30.260
But we have one that's called
Cloud Identity, in fact,

00:16:30.260 --> 00:16:31.930
where we manage
things like users,

00:16:31.930 --> 00:16:33.710
groups, role information.

00:16:33.710 --> 00:16:34.710
That's all very obvious.

00:16:34.710 --> 00:16:36.345
They all sort of do that.

00:16:36.345 --> 00:16:38.720
Now, imagine a world where
that identity system is locked

00:16:38.720 --> 00:16:39.750
into your calendar, right?

00:16:39.750 --> 00:16:41.458
So you understand
somebody's on vacation.

00:16:41.458 --> 00:16:43.717
Somebody is in one
office and not another.

00:16:43.717 --> 00:16:45.800
And a request is coming
in from somewhere strange.

00:16:45.800 --> 00:16:48.708
You can start to kind of
create these sort of vectors.

00:16:48.708 --> 00:16:50.750
The other thing is you
need a massive system that

00:16:50.750 --> 00:16:52.040
does login abuse detection.

00:16:52.040 --> 00:16:54.690
For us, the same system we
use for the enterprise side

00:16:54.690 --> 00:16:56.190
is what we use on
the consumer side.

00:16:56.190 --> 00:16:58.640
So we are getting good at
detecting fraudulent signals

00:16:58.640 --> 00:17:02.390
because we do 1.2 billion
unique logins a month.

00:17:02.390 --> 00:17:04.250
And so, we've gotten
very good at detecting

00:17:04.250 --> 00:17:05.960
what those signals are.

00:17:05.960 --> 00:17:07.293
Those are pretty obvious things.

00:17:07.293 --> 00:17:09.252
The two other things I'll
just touch on briefly

00:17:09.252 --> 00:17:11.170
that we find that a lot
of customers don't do

00:17:11.170 --> 00:17:12.230
is session control.

00:17:12.230 --> 00:17:14.119
I don't mean infinite
versus one hour.

00:17:14.119 --> 00:17:16.430
I mean that every application
has a different use

00:17:16.430 --> 00:17:17.700
case or a different footprint.

00:17:17.700 --> 00:17:19.950
And you should be able to
create unique session length

00:17:19.950 --> 00:17:22.310
controls for each of your
types of applications

00:17:22.310 --> 00:17:23.640
that are out there.

00:17:23.640 --> 00:17:25.010
The other one is MFA.

00:17:25.010 --> 00:17:27.170
I'll say this.

00:17:27.170 --> 00:17:29.630
MFA is better than
no MFA, but there

00:17:29.630 --> 00:17:31.130
is a better story than OTP.

00:17:31.130 --> 00:17:35.150
If you don't believe me,
just Bing OTP phishing.

00:17:35.150 --> 00:17:39.800
Or you can Google it if you
want, CM vendor agnostic.

00:17:39.800 --> 00:17:42.860
But no, in seriousness, the
OTP code have been phished.

00:17:42.860 --> 00:17:45.030
You can do a simple
search for it and see.

00:17:45.030 --> 00:17:48.420
What we use internally is
this idea of a FIDO U2F key.

00:17:48.420 --> 00:17:49.190
Use any vendor.

00:17:49.190 --> 00:17:50.232
It doesn't have to be us.

00:17:50.232 --> 00:17:51.020
It can be anybody.

00:17:51.020 --> 00:17:52.520
We find that this makes it--

00:17:52.520 --> 00:17:55.680
if I say un-phish-able, you know
that's always a dangerous thing

00:17:55.680 --> 00:17:56.180
to say.

00:17:56.180 --> 00:17:57.800
So I'll say very, very
phishing resistant.

00:17:57.800 --> 00:17:59.175
And no credentials
at Google have

00:17:59.175 --> 00:18:00.650
been phished in
the last 18 months,

00:18:00.650 --> 00:18:03.030
because we've moved
to this model.

00:18:03.030 --> 00:18:04.623
The second portion of this--

00:18:04.623 --> 00:18:06.040
so we talked about
Cloud identity.

00:18:06.040 --> 00:18:07.910
The second portion is
actually the device itself.

00:18:07.910 --> 00:18:09.330
Here we offer a few solutions.

00:18:09.330 --> 00:18:11.330
The one I'm going to talk
about today, because I think

00:18:11.330 --> 00:18:13.372
we have a broader story
that's in other sessions,

00:18:13.372 --> 00:18:15.380
is what we call
Endpoint Verification.

00:18:15.380 --> 00:18:19.070
Endpoint Verification is really
a simple Chrome browser-based

00:18:19.070 --> 00:18:23.890
and soon other browsers,
preempting Samuel's complaints,

00:18:23.890 --> 00:18:26.253
where we understand the
security posture of a device,

00:18:26.253 --> 00:18:27.920
where we understand
the disc encryption,

00:18:27.920 --> 00:18:29.120
the screen lock
version, the version

00:18:29.120 --> 00:18:30.580
of software running
on the device,

00:18:30.580 --> 00:18:32.883
secured boot, all these
different kinds of things.

00:18:32.883 --> 00:18:33.800
Those are interesting.

00:18:33.800 --> 00:18:35.480
I think what you'll find--

00:18:35.480 --> 00:18:38.690
my friend Sanjay, Sanjay
Poonen, today on the stage

00:18:38.690 --> 00:18:39.488
kind of announced.

00:18:39.488 --> 00:18:41.030
He kind of pre-announced
this for us.

00:18:41.030 --> 00:18:42.197
We are working with vendors.

00:18:42.197 --> 00:18:45.170
So if you already have a mobile
device management or endpoint

00:18:45.170 --> 00:18:47.270
management system, we
are working together

00:18:47.270 --> 00:18:50.000
with this whole ecosystem to
feed their signals into this.

00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:51.860
So you can say
Vendor X or Partner

00:18:51.860 --> 00:18:53.445
Co is feeding those signals in.

00:18:53.445 --> 00:18:55.820
I want to use that to make
access decisions for my Google

00:18:55.820 --> 00:18:57.920
resources or resources on AWS.

00:18:57.920 --> 00:18:59.078
You can certainly do that.

00:18:59.078 --> 00:19:00.620
And the other thing
is you want to be

00:19:00.620 --> 00:19:02.578
able to distinguish
between corp-issued devices

00:19:02.578 --> 00:19:04.100
and non-corp-issued devices.

00:19:04.100 --> 00:19:06.000
And so I think Gagan will give a
little bit of a preview of some

00:19:06.000 --> 00:19:07.260
of what we're doing there.

00:19:07.260 --> 00:19:08.870
But the idea should be
that not every device

00:19:08.870 --> 00:19:11.037
should be cut off just
because it's not corp-issued.

00:19:11.037 --> 00:19:15.007
It just maybe they can't
access really secure systems.

00:19:15.007 --> 00:19:17.090
And then, what you get for
free when you run on us

00:19:17.090 --> 00:19:18.710
is this idea of a
Google Front End.

00:19:18.710 --> 00:19:21.800
And this is a
globally-distributed, near zero

00:19:21.800 --> 00:19:24.183
latency frontend that
does a few things for you.

00:19:24.183 --> 00:19:26.600
One is it collects all the
context of the request, the IP,

00:19:26.600 --> 00:19:28.120
the location, the time of day.

00:19:28.120 --> 00:19:30.620
But it also gives you lots of
things for free, if you will--

00:19:30.620 --> 00:19:32.840
things like DDoS
protection, enforcing TLS,

00:19:32.840 --> 00:19:33.965
the latest versions of TLS.

00:19:33.965 --> 00:19:35.548
These are the kinds
of things that you

00:19:35.548 --> 00:19:37.520
need any system be able
to handle and anywhere

00:19:37.520 --> 00:19:39.110
in the world.

00:19:39.110 --> 00:19:40.850
And then, the next
talk of this is

00:19:40.850 --> 00:19:43.047
what we call the
Access Context Manager.

00:19:43.047 --> 00:19:44.630
If you remember, we
talked about rules

00:19:44.630 --> 00:19:46.045
that you should
be able to apply.

00:19:46.045 --> 00:19:47.420
The Access Context
Manager allows

00:19:47.420 --> 00:19:48.795
you to create
these levels, where

00:19:48.795 --> 00:19:52.190
you can kind of pair together
these various context things.

00:19:52.190 --> 00:19:54.440
So you can say a device
that's running only the latest

00:19:54.440 --> 00:19:55.640
version of OS.

00:19:55.640 --> 00:19:57.840
And coming in from this
IP address, for example,

00:19:57.840 --> 00:19:59.840
should be able to access
my most secure systems.

00:19:59.840 --> 00:20:03.110
Anything that's n minus 2 in
terms of OS version and maybe

00:20:03.110 --> 00:20:04.610
doesn't need second
factor so often,

00:20:04.610 --> 00:20:06.830
we'll allow to touch
second tier systems.

00:20:06.830 --> 00:20:08.060
You should be able to
create rules like that

00:20:08.060 --> 00:20:09.810
and make them mass
appliable, if you will,

00:20:09.810 --> 00:20:11.200
to all of your systems.

00:20:11.200 --> 00:20:12.950
Many of you have-- you
work in enterprises

00:20:12.950 --> 00:20:15.117
where you're running
thousands, literally thousands,

00:20:15.117 --> 00:20:16.220
of IT applications.

00:20:16.220 --> 00:20:18.595
And you don't want take the
time to go through one by one

00:20:18.595 --> 00:20:19.880
and tag these things.

00:20:19.880 --> 00:20:23.180
The Access Contact Manager is
really our path to get there.

00:20:23.180 --> 00:20:24.680
And then, you took
all this context.

00:20:24.680 --> 00:20:25.790
You created all these rules.

00:20:25.790 --> 00:20:27.080
And now the key is
you have to enforce it

00:20:27.080 --> 00:20:28.700
against all of your endpoints.

00:20:28.700 --> 00:20:31.340
I think what you'll find is we
have several solutions here,

00:20:31.340 --> 00:20:32.798
depending on what
your use case is.

00:20:32.798 --> 00:20:34.382
And Samuel talked
about some of these,

00:20:34.382 --> 00:20:36.030
but I'll just briefly
touch on them.

00:20:36.030 --> 00:20:38.420
The first one is something
called Cloud IAP,

00:20:38.420 --> 00:20:39.410
Identity-Aware Proxy.

00:20:39.410 --> 00:20:41.000
So if you're writing
a web application

00:20:41.000 --> 00:20:44.120
on GCP, or in another
cloud, or on-prem,

00:20:44.120 --> 00:20:46.370
you should be able to protect
that through these rules

00:20:46.370 --> 00:20:47.690
that you've now defined.

00:20:47.690 --> 00:20:48.860
You should also be able to
do that for, by the way,

00:20:48.860 --> 00:20:50.660
SSH and TCP connections as well.

00:20:50.660 --> 00:20:52.903
And the Identity-Aware
Proxy does exactly that.

00:20:52.903 --> 00:20:55.070
And it does what it's clever
name tells you it does.

00:20:55.070 --> 00:20:56.150
It really looks at the identity.

00:20:56.150 --> 00:20:57.150
It looks at the context.

00:20:57.150 --> 00:20:58.567
And it makes the
access decisions.

00:20:58.567 --> 00:21:00.233
The other thing we
would allow you to do

00:21:00.233 --> 00:21:02.360
is the same sort of thing
with our Cloud IAM system

00:21:02.360 --> 00:21:03.938
for, think of resources.

00:21:03.938 --> 00:21:05.480
You have a storage
bucket, let's just

00:21:05.480 --> 00:21:07.610
say that's holding a lot
of personal information,

00:21:07.610 --> 00:21:10.580
you can create context awareness
around access to those requests

00:21:10.580 --> 00:21:11.460
as well.

00:21:11.460 --> 00:21:13.140
That's just an example.

00:21:13.140 --> 00:21:15.200
You can do all of this
with our G Suite products

00:21:15.200 --> 00:21:17.088
through our Cloud
Identity product line.

00:21:17.088 --> 00:21:19.130
So you can say, I want to
be able to access Gmail

00:21:19.130 --> 00:21:21.338
at this time of day, or only
from a corporate device,

00:21:21.338 --> 00:21:23.130
or drive, or whatever
it happens to be.

00:21:23.130 --> 00:21:25.338
And then one of the areas
I think we really stand out

00:21:25.338 --> 00:21:28.400
from the other vendors is what
we call VPC Service Controls.

00:21:28.400 --> 00:21:30.650
This is the idea that
every public cloud is going

00:21:30.650 --> 00:21:32.053
to have their APIs on the web.

00:21:32.053 --> 00:21:33.470
That's just the
nature of a cloud.

00:21:33.470 --> 00:21:35.470
That's, in fact, what
clouds are supposed to do.

00:21:35.470 --> 00:21:38.240
But it's terrifying this idea
that an employee, who suddenly

00:21:38.240 --> 00:21:40.460
goes rogue, or even
unintentionally,

00:21:40.460 --> 00:21:41.990
takes their device
to a coffee shop

00:21:41.990 --> 00:21:43.615
and just decides to
start shutting down

00:21:43.615 --> 00:21:44.600
production systems.

00:21:44.600 --> 00:21:46.850
For all the other types of
things we're talking about,

00:21:46.850 --> 00:21:49.347
this should be the most
terrifying to most people.

00:21:49.347 --> 00:21:50.930
And today we now we
now have a product

00:21:50.930 --> 00:21:53.097
that's in GA that's called
the VPC Service Controls.

00:21:53.097 --> 00:21:55.303
That allows-- that will
today take starts--

00:21:55.303 --> 00:21:56.845
it has the beginnings
of this, but it

00:21:56.845 --> 00:21:59.300
will start to take all
of the same context

00:21:59.300 --> 00:22:01.400
and allow you to apply
it to public APIs.

00:22:01.400 --> 00:22:03.890
Think of like storage buckets
and VM productions and things

00:22:03.890 --> 00:22:04.477
like that.

00:22:04.477 --> 00:22:06.560
You'll be able to apply
these same controls there.

00:22:06.560 --> 00:22:08.670
And more will be
coming as we go along.

00:22:08.670 --> 00:22:10.460
So I think it's one
thing to talk about it

00:22:10.460 --> 00:22:11.940
and more interesting
to attempt to demo it.

00:22:11.940 --> 00:22:14.300
So I'll have Gagan come up
and go through some demos.

00:22:18.300 --> 00:22:20.300
[APPLAUSE]

00:22:21.300 --> 00:22:23.160
GAGAN ARORA: Thank you, Ameet.

00:22:23.160 --> 00:22:24.040
Hello, everyone.

00:22:24.040 --> 00:22:26.670
My name is Gagan Arora,
and I'm a product manager

00:22:26.670 --> 00:22:28.770
in Google Cloud.

00:22:28.770 --> 00:22:31.350
As Ameet just touched
on, context-aware access

00:22:31.350 --> 00:22:34.680
is the key pillar for
BeyondCorp security model.

00:22:34.680 --> 00:22:37.980
And you all can imagine
a lot of use cases

00:22:37.980 --> 00:22:39.600
when you think of context.

00:22:39.600 --> 00:22:41.850
So imagine that you
want to control access

00:22:41.850 --> 00:22:45.480
to your resources based on
where the user is coming from

00:22:45.480 --> 00:22:49.325
or what you know about their
device, how secure that is.

00:22:49.325 --> 00:22:51.450
There are so many use cases
that we can talk about,

00:22:51.450 --> 00:22:53.190
but over the next
few minutes, I'm

00:22:53.190 --> 00:22:55.980
going to go through
two specific use cases.

00:22:55.980 --> 00:22:58.890
One on the GCP side
and one on G Suite side

00:22:58.890 --> 00:23:02.280
to highlight how you can
enable context-aware access

00:23:02.280 --> 00:23:03.780
in your organization.

00:23:03.780 --> 00:23:05.820
After that, Sam is
going to come in again.

00:23:05.820 --> 00:23:09.720
And he's going to talk about how
they are deploying Google tools

00:23:09.720 --> 00:23:11.130
to address their use cases.

00:23:15.910 --> 00:23:17.670
So let's look at
the use case one.

00:23:17.670 --> 00:23:20.640
So this is something that
we heard over and over

00:23:20.640 --> 00:23:23.730
from our customers-- that
I want to allow access

00:23:23.730 --> 00:23:26.580
to all my applications to
users from wherever they are.

00:23:26.580 --> 00:23:29.880
But there is an HR
application that I may have,

00:23:29.880 --> 00:23:33.780
where I want to restrict
access to contractors only

00:23:33.780 --> 00:23:36.600
if they are coming from
a corporate location.

00:23:36.600 --> 00:23:38.445
So let's see how that
works in our scenario.

00:23:40.950 --> 00:23:43.980
So since this is an
HR application on GCP,

00:23:43.980 --> 00:23:47.070
an end user can access
this application

00:23:47.070 --> 00:23:48.720
from wherever they are.

00:23:48.720 --> 00:23:51.180
Now the admin wants to
enforce the use case

00:23:51.180 --> 00:23:52.750
that we just described.

00:23:52.750 --> 00:23:55.050
And essentially, I want
to restrict the access

00:23:55.050 --> 00:23:58.440
based on the location
of contractors network.

00:23:58.440 --> 00:24:00.330
So I go into Access
Context Manager,

00:24:00.330 --> 00:24:02.940
create a rule
called access level.

00:24:02.940 --> 00:24:06.490
And I define the contractor
IP address first.

00:24:06.490 --> 00:24:08.970
The next thing I want to do
is, since this application is

00:24:08.970 --> 00:24:13.410
behind the load balancer, I
want to enable IAP for this.

00:24:13.410 --> 00:24:15.930
And this allows me
to configure who

00:24:15.930 --> 00:24:18.450
can get access to this
application and under what

00:24:18.450 --> 00:24:19.780
conditions.

00:24:19.780 --> 00:24:21.360
So in this case,
I am giving access

00:24:21.360 --> 00:24:25.860
to Bob, who is my contractor
but only if he is coming

00:24:25.860 --> 00:24:29.020
from his corporate network.

00:24:29.020 --> 00:24:31.170
So once I set this
rule, I can go back

00:24:31.170 --> 00:24:36.180
and check and make sure that Bob
has access to this application.

00:24:36.180 --> 00:24:38.790
And now we look at it
from Bob's scenario

00:24:38.790 --> 00:24:41.640
when he is trying to
access this HR application.

00:24:41.640 --> 00:24:45.060
So in the first scenario,
Bob is in office.

00:24:45.060 --> 00:24:47.180
And he is logged
into his device.

00:24:47.180 --> 00:24:49.800
And he is able to
access this application,

00:24:49.800 --> 00:24:52.450
because he is on the
corporate network.

00:24:52.450 --> 00:24:54.990
Now let's say, Bob
goes to a coffee shop

00:24:54.990 --> 00:24:56.730
to get a cup of coffee.

00:24:56.730 --> 00:24:59.020
And he tries to access
that application again.

00:24:59.020 --> 00:25:02.960
And in that case, because he is
not from the corporate network,

00:25:02.960 --> 00:25:07.000
he's still logged in,
but he is denied access.

00:25:07.000 --> 00:25:09.390
So as you can see, it's
fairly easy for you

00:25:09.390 --> 00:25:12.270
to set up these kind of
contextual rules, that

00:25:12.270 --> 00:25:15.390
allow you to restrict access
to the applications, that

00:25:15.390 --> 00:25:19.620
may reside on GCP, on-prem,
or any other cloud.

00:25:19.620 --> 00:25:23.610
Now let's touch on the
case for G Suite side.

00:25:23.610 --> 00:25:25.680
This is, again, a
fairly common scenario

00:25:25.680 --> 00:25:28.500
that we see among
our customers, where

00:25:28.500 --> 00:25:31.590
I am a customer with lot of
sensitive data in my Drive

00:25:31.590 --> 00:25:32.430
and Docs.

00:25:32.430 --> 00:25:35.310
So think of Docs, Sheets,
and anything else.

00:25:35.310 --> 00:25:37.500
I want to make
sure that users who

00:25:37.500 --> 00:25:39.900
are coming to access
this Drive and Docs

00:25:39.900 --> 00:25:43.440
are coming in only if
they have a secure device.

00:25:43.440 --> 00:25:45.270
And I'll walk you
through exactly how you

00:25:45.270 --> 00:25:48.620
can define your version
of secure device

00:25:48.620 --> 00:25:49.670
over the next few steps.

00:25:52.220 --> 00:25:55.060
The first thing you need to do,
or your end users need to have,

00:25:55.060 --> 00:25:56.960
is something called
Endpoint Verification.

00:25:56.960 --> 00:25:59.200
It's a Chrome plugin,
that either they

00:25:59.200 --> 00:26:02.440
can install themselves,
or you, as an admin,

00:26:02.440 --> 00:26:05.540
can push it to their devices.

00:26:05.540 --> 00:26:10.000
Once the end users have
Endpoint Verification installed,

00:26:10.000 --> 00:26:13.245
as an admin, you go to your
familiar admin console.

00:26:13.245 --> 00:26:14.620
In your security
settings, you'll

00:26:14.620 --> 00:26:18.010
see a tab now called
Context-Aware Access.

00:26:18.010 --> 00:26:20.800
Once you click on it, you'll
be able to set different access

00:26:20.800 --> 00:26:23.720
levels to define the conditions.

00:26:23.720 --> 00:26:27.292
And then you can apply them
to different resources.

00:26:27.292 --> 00:26:29.500
So in this case, as you can
see, the corporate office

00:26:29.500 --> 00:26:31.610
IAP that we defined
shows up here.

00:26:31.610 --> 00:26:36.680
But we're going to define a
new rule called Secure Device.

00:26:36.680 --> 00:26:38.690
Once you've created,
you are able to choose

00:26:38.690 --> 00:26:40.190
different attributes
that you can

00:26:40.190 --> 00:26:43.280
use to define what a
secure device means

00:26:43.280 --> 00:26:45.870
for your organization.

00:26:45.870 --> 00:26:48.800
In this case, I pick
three specific attributes.

00:26:48.800 --> 00:26:52.415
One, I want to make sure that
any device accessing my Drive

00:26:52.415 --> 00:26:55.670
and Docs has a password on.

00:26:55.670 --> 00:27:00.080
Number two, I want to make sure
that the device is encrypted.

00:27:00.080 --> 00:27:02.120
And number three,
I want to make sure

00:27:02.120 --> 00:27:04.640
that the device is
fairly up-to-date.

00:27:04.640 --> 00:27:07.190
And in this case, I define
that the macOS version

00:27:07.190 --> 00:27:10.610
should be 10.0.0, which is
a fairly high version that I

00:27:10.610 --> 00:27:12.830
want to define.

00:27:12.830 --> 00:27:14.720
Once you create
this access level,

00:27:14.720 --> 00:27:17.270
it's going to show up in the
list of all access levels

00:27:17.270 --> 00:27:18.470
that you have defined.

00:27:18.470 --> 00:27:21.320
And you can choose
other attributes like,

00:27:21.320 --> 00:27:23.120
is their device
corporate owned or not,

00:27:23.120 --> 00:27:25.520
is it a Chrome OS
device which I trust

00:27:25.520 --> 00:27:28.980
and has verified access
on, and so on and so forth.

00:27:28.980 --> 00:27:30.932
So once you have defined
the access level,

00:27:30.932 --> 00:27:33.515
the next step is to actually go
and assign those access levels

00:27:33.515 --> 00:27:35.900
to resources you
want to protect.

00:27:35.900 --> 00:27:38.300
And you can do it on their
entire domain that you have,

00:27:38.300 --> 00:27:40.840
or you can pick a
subset of users,

00:27:40.840 --> 00:27:44.450
what we call organization
unit, and apply the same rules

00:27:44.450 --> 00:27:46.290
to only a subset of users.

00:27:46.290 --> 00:27:49.460
So in this case, I apply
these rules to Drive and Docs

00:27:49.460 --> 00:27:51.240
for contractors.

00:27:51.240 --> 00:27:55.340
And I pick that any contractor
who is accessing Drive and Docs

00:27:55.340 --> 00:27:57.680
should be coming
with a secure device.

00:28:02.570 --> 00:28:04.610
So once I have defined
this, any contractor

00:28:04.610 --> 00:28:08.450
who does not meet the minimum
criteria that I have set

00:28:08.450 --> 00:28:11.900
is not going to be able to
access the Drive and Docs that

00:28:11.900 --> 00:28:13.710
belong to my organization.

00:28:13.710 --> 00:28:16.600
So let's see, again, we go
back to our contractor, Bob,

00:28:16.600 --> 00:28:19.520
who is trying to access
this application.

00:28:19.520 --> 00:28:22.360
He does have Endpoint
Verification installed.

00:28:22.360 --> 00:28:24.860
And this is something that sends
the device information back

00:28:24.860 --> 00:28:28.290
to us, where we can check
for contextual information.

00:28:28.290 --> 00:28:31.430
The next thing we check is
the OS version of the device

00:28:31.430 --> 00:28:33.080
that Bob has.

00:28:33.080 --> 00:28:37.610
He has a version of 10.13.6,
so this is higher than 10.0.0

00:28:37.610 --> 00:28:38.630
that I've set.

00:28:38.630 --> 00:28:42.260
So in this case, Bob should be
able to access Drive and Docs

00:28:42.260 --> 00:28:46.170
when he tries to
access his documents.

00:28:46.170 --> 00:28:49.370
Now imagine a security
vulnerability has come out.

00:28:49.370 --> 00:28:51.260
And Apple has
released a new patch.

00:28:51.260 --> 00:28:52.970
And you want all
your users to be

00:28:52.970 --> 00:28:55.370
coming from this latest
patch when they're accessing

00:28:55.370 --> 00:28:57.560
this sensitive documentation.

00:28:57.560 --> 00:28:59.210
All you have to
do in this case is

00:28:59.210 --> 00:29:02.000
to go back to your access
level that you defined,

00:29:02.000 --> 00:29:06.520
update the OS version that
you defined, 10.0.0, to,

00:29:06.520 --> 00:29:09.020
let's say, 10.14.2.

00:29:09.020 --> 00:29:10.760
And all this happens
behind the scenes

00:29:10.760 --> 00:29:14.540
where it gets trickled down as a
policy to all the users and all

00:29:14.540 --> 00:29:16.570
the applications that
it was applied to.

00:29:16.570 --> 00:29:20.510
And in this case, when Bob tries
to access the same application

00:29:20.510 --> 00:29:24.000
again, he will be denied
access, because his OS

00:29:24.000 --> 00:29:29.720
version, 10.13.6, is lower than
10.14.2 that you, as an admin,

00:29:29.720 --> 00:29:31.240
require.

00:29:31.240 --> 00:29:32.810
So as you can see,
it's fairly simple

00:29:32.810 --> 00:29:37.130
to apply these rules across your
organization on GCP, G Suite.

00:29:37.130 --> 00:29:39.200
You pick your
attributes and signals

00:29:39.200 --> 00:29:41.310
that you want to
use that in context.

00:29:41.310 --> 00:29:43.140
And then apply these rules.

00:29:43.140 --> 00:29:45.890
We also have a live demo
running on your right side,

00:29:45.890 --> 00:29:47.180
so feel free to go there.

00:29:47.180 --> 00:29:49.430
And we'd love to hear
more about your use cases

00:29:49.430 --> 00:29:53.060
and how context-aware access
can solve it for you guys.

00:29:53.060 --> 00:29:54.770
And with that, I'll
invite Sam back.

00:29:54.770 --> 00:29:57.170
And he's going to
talk more about IAP

00:29:57.170 --> 00:29:59.660
and context-aware access
and how they have deployed

00:29:59.660 --> 00:30:01.630
this to solve their use case.

00:30:01.630 --> 00:30:03.055
[APPLAUSE]

00:30:04.005 --> 00:30:07.330
SAMUEL KEELEY: Thank you.

00:30:07.330 --> 00:30:13.110
So when IAP was first announced
a couple of years ago,

00:30:13.110 --> 00:30:17.410
I was pretty excited by
it because we already

00:30:17.410 --> 00:30:18.950
used G Suite, and
we already used

00:30:18.950 --> 00:30:22.370
Cloud Identity for our users.

00:30:22.370 --> 00:30:26.630
So having users that would
already integrate with this

00:30:26.630 --> 00:30:29.550
was pretty cool
and pretty useful.

00:30:29.550 --> 00:30:34.280
But the question was could
it help us solve our problems

00:30:34.280 --> 00:30:37.040
with our user experience like,
making the user experience

00:30:37.040 --> 00:30:38.760
better?

00:30:38.760 --> 00:30:41.430
We had a pretty good grasp
on user authentication.

00:30:41.430 --> 00:30:43.795
But just authenticating
was more than we needed.

00:30:43.795 --> 00:30:45.920
What we really cared about
was device side as well,

00:30:45.920 --> 00:30:49.550
especially since we've invested
so much in our endpoints

00:30:49.550 --> 00:30:52.260
over the last three years.

00:30:52.260 --> 00:30:55.010
So we tried other
authenticated proxies.

00:30:55.010 --> 00:30:58.940
And I personally use some
in the past, usually labeled

00:30:58.940 --> 00:31:01.550
as clientless VPN.

00:31:01.550 --> 00:31:04.610
But they just kind of ended
up causing more problems

00:31:04.610 --> 00:31:07.820
than they purported to solve.

00:31:07.820 --> 00:31:09.660
So we needed
something different.

00:31:09.660 --> 00:31:13.250
And that would enhance
our security and our user

00:31:13.250 --> 00:31:14.750
experience.

00:31:14.750 --> 00:31:17.480
With IAP working as
part of a load balancer,

00:31:17.480 --> 00:31:21.380
it seemed to kind of fit
how modern apps work.

00:31:21.380 --> 00:31:26.150
So setting up the context,
there were three things

00:31:26.150 --> 00:31:27.840
that we were trying
to achieve here.

00:31:27.840 --> 00:31:29.780
One was consistent access.

00:31:29.780 --> 00:31:33.620
So regardless of
where somebody was,

00:31:33.620 --> 00:31:35.840
they should be able to
access the applications

00:31:35.840 --> 00:31:39.590
that they need to do their
job, through one easy method.

00:31:39.590 --> 00:31:46.550
So where in the past,
we've had 802.1X, VPN,

00:31:46.550 --> 00:31:50.990
and other methods to
get to an application,

00:31:50.990 --> 00:31:54.980
it ends up confusing users,
where sometimes they need VPN

00:31:54.980 --> 00:31:58.490
for something, sometimes
they need to 802.1X and VPN

00:31:58.490 --> 00:31:59.780
for something.

00:31:59.780 --> 00:32:04.980
And it just-- it presents a
large confusion to our users.

00:32:04.980 --> 00:32:09.560
So we wanted consistent
access per application.

00:32:09.560 --> 00:32:13.220
We knew that we were still going
to need VPN for some things.

00:32:13.220 --> 00:32:18.530
But for each application, the
access should be consistent.

00:32:18.530 --> 00:32:21.950
We wanted to enhance application
performance, especially

00:32:21.950 --> 00:32:25.880
for users that are far
away from these apps.

00:32:25.880 --> 00:32:30.170
So we have thousands of users
that are thousands of miles

00:32:30.170 --> 00:32:32.780
away from the applications
that they're using.

00:32:32.780 --> 00:32:36.380
And while it would be wonderful
if every application that we

00:32:36.380 --> 00:32:40.520
use supported
multi-region deployments,

00:32:40.520 --> 00:32:44.870
that's just not the
reality, especially when

00:32:44.870 --> 00:32:46.520
we're buying third-party apps.

00:32:46.520 --> 00:32:50.043
They might be able to work
in an HA configuration,

00:32:50.043 --> 00:32:51.710
but they're going to
want to be deployed

00:32:51.710 --> 00:32:52.838
all next to each other.

00:32:52.838 --> 00:32:55.130
So we're just going to have
to pick a region for an app

00:32:55.130 --> 00:32:57.360
to run in and deal with that.

00:32:57.360 --> 00:32:59.720
So we want to enhance
application performance,

00:32:59.720 --> 00:33:04.370
have the best networking
that we could.

00:33:04.370 --> 00:33:10.370
And lastly, because security is
important, but maybe shouldn't

00:33:10.370 --> 00:33:13.990
be the first thing on your
mind for everything out there,

00:33:13.990 --> 00:33:18.710
is that we wanted to enforce
the endpoint security

00:33:18.710 --> 00:33:21.540
state that we've implemented
over the past two years.

00:33:21.540 --> 00:33:24.230
So we're really
happy with the way

00:33:24.230 --> 00:33:27.920
that our Mac, Windows, and
Chrome OS endpoints are

00:33:27.920 --> 00:33:29.810
managed.

00:33:29.810 --> 00:33:32.420
But if we're not
actually enforcing

00:33:32.420 --> 00:33:36.330
that those devices are the ones
connecting to our applications,

00:33:36.330 --> 00:33:40.290
I don't know if there is truly
a point to us having done that.

00:33:40.290 --> 00:33:42.820
So the first thing
that you're going

00:33:42.820 --> 00:33:46.940
to run into if you want to
use context-aware access

00:33:46.940 --> 00:33:49.550
is the Endpoint
Verification extension.

00:33:49.550 --> 00:33:53.630
And the data that this
extension gives you

00:33:53.630 --> 00:33:57.990
is incredibly powerful
and incredibly valuable.

00:33:57.990 --> 00:34:05.960
So we actually deployed this
extension almost two years ago.

00:34:05.960 --> 00:34:10.460
And it's been collecting
data for us ever since.

00:34:10.460 --> 00:34:13.699
So one of the problems
that companies

00:34:13.699 --> 00:34:17.389
run into over their growth
is that their inventory,

00:34:17.389 --> 00:34:21.679
their physical inventory, isn't
as good as they think it is.

00:34:21.679 --> 00:34:24.540
So when you hand a
laptop to somebody,

00:34:24.540 --> 00:34:26.540
the first thing you're
doing when you're growing

00:34:26.540 --> 00:34:28.498
is you're probably going
to have a spreadsheet.

00:34:28.498 --> 00:34:30.770
And you're going to say
who you assigned it to.

00:34:30.770 --> 00:34:35.179
And then you'll grow into some
physical inventory management

00:34:35.179 --> 00:34:35.989
system.

00:34:35.989 --> 00:34:38.960
But that data in there is
only as good as the data

00:34:38.960 --> 00:34:40.100
that you put in there.

00:34:40.100 --> 00:34:41.820
And most of that's
going to be manual.

00:34:41.820 --> 00:34:44.989
And that doesn't scale to
having thousands and thousands

00:34:44.989 --> 00:34:46.250
of endpoints.

00:34:46.250 --> 00:34:53.090
So before we went and
used IAP for anything,

00:34:53.090 --> 00:34:54.679
we rolled this extension out.

00:34:54.679 --> 00:34:59.070
And it will tell you who's
actually using your devices.

00:34:59.070 --> 00:35:00.500
So we force install
this extension

00:35:00.500 --> 00:35:03.740
on all of our managed
devices and found

00:35:03.740 --> 00:35:07.640
that definitely less
than 100% of our devices

00:35:07.640 --> 00:35:10.250
were assigned to the people
who were actually using them.

00:35:10.250 --> 00:35:14.740
So here's a list of my
devices that I'm using.

00:35:14.740 --> 00:35:16.753
And some of them
are company owned.

00:35:16.753 --> 00:35:17.920
Some of them are user owned.

00:35:17.920 --> 00:35:21.610
And here's an example
of my MacBook Pro.

00:35:21.610 --> 00:35:24.350
And it shows this
basic security state.

00:35:24.350 --> 00:35:25.370
It shows company owned.

00:35:25.370 --> 00:35:29.125
And we can use that data to
inform our physical inventory.

00:35:29.125 --> 00:35:32.380
And we can also use that to
inform context-aware access.

00:35:32.380 --> 00:35:34.750
So here's the thing.

00:35:34.750 --> 00:35:39.310
We don't use GCP to
run our applications.

00:35:39.310 --> 00:35:44.740
Like I said before,
Airbnb.com runs all on AWS.

00:35:44.740 --> 00:35:49.300
And we have some applications
that run on-prem as well.

00:35:49.300 --> 00:35:53.680
But IAP with context-aware was
still very appealing to us,

00:35:53.680 --> 00:35:57.440
because our users exists on
Google, our internal users.

00:35:57.440 --> 00:36:02.770
So how could we
achieve this when

00:36:02.770 --> 00:36:05.290
IAP runs on the load balancer,
and the load balancer can't

00:36:05.290 --> 00:36:10.510
target apps which
run outside of GCP?

00:36:10.510 --> 00:36:15.890
So this is what the diagram
of this ended up looking like.

00:36:15.890 --> 00:36:20.770
So we have our users, who talk
to the load balancer, which

00:36:20.770 --> 00:36:24.220
goes to and talks to IAP.

00:36:24.220 --> 00:36:30.820
And behind the load
balancer, we have just Nginx.

00:36:30.820 --> 00:36:35.500
And we've-- yeah, so
we've set up Nginx,

00:36:35.500 --> 00:36:39.850
and that routes the traffic all
the way back to the third-party

00:36:39.850 --> 00:36:42.550
apps running in EC2.

00:36:42.550 --> 00:36:46.600
So thankfully, when we built--
when we rebuilt our network

00:36:46.600 --> 00:36:48.130
over the last few
years, we kind of

00:36:48.130 --> 00:36:52.680
anticipated that we'd be
connecting to more than just

00:36:52.680 --> 00:36:54.542
AWS in the future.

00:36:54.542 --> 00:36:56.500
Now we weren't really
thinking about connecting

00:36:56.500 --> 00:36:58.090
to other clouds.

00:36:58.090 --> 00:36:59.680
But we knew that
we'd probably have

00:36:59.680 --> 00:37:04.430
some like IPSec VPNs going to
vendors or something like that.

00:37:04.430 --> 00:37:07.690
So getting a Partner
Interconnect setup

00:37:07.690 --> 00:37:10.990
to land right next to the AWS
Direct Connect that we're using

00:37:10.990 --> 00:37:15.310
was probably one of the
easiest parts of this project.

00:37:15.310 --> 00:37:18.880
And the only thing that we've
had to actually deploy in GCP

00:37:18.880 --> 00:37:23.170
is this Nginx instance,
or Nginx instances really.

00:37:23.170 --> 00:37:25.750
But you could very well use
a different kind of proxy.

00:37:25.750 --> 00:37:27.727
You could use Envoy
if you wanted to.

00:37:27.727 --> 00:37:28.810
That's the cool new thing.

00:37:28.810 --> 00:37:31.540
You could even deploy
that in Kubernetes.

00:37:31.540 --> 00:37:34.900
But we have a lot of
familiarity with Nginx,

00:37:34.900 --> 00:37:36.197
so we're using that.

00:37:36.197 --> 00:37:38.155
And then, it goes over
the Partner Interconnect

00:37:38.155 --> 00:37:41.680
and ends up back
at the application.

00:37:41.680 --> 00:37:43.680
So that's pretty easy.

00:37:43.680 --> 00:37:45.900
It's fairly straightforward.

00:37:45.900 --> 00:37:48.370
Nginx just runs as
far as the traffic.

00:37:48.370 --> 00:37:49.900
Users can get to the apps.

00:37:49.900 --> 00:37:53.090
We can use Endpoint
Verification.

00:37:53.090 --> 00:37:59.260
And that informs the BeyondCorp,
which acts as context manager

00:37:59.260 --> 00:38:00.400
references.

00:38:00.400 --> 00:38:03.280
And then we also have
Osquery and other endpoint

00:38:03.280 --> 00:38:06.130
instrumentation, along with
our physical inventory,

00:38:06.130 --> 00:38:09.820
talking to StreamAlert and
then talking to Lambdas,

00:38:09.820 --> 00:38:14.010
that we've written,
that talk back to GCP,

00:38:14.010 --> 00:38:18.040
that talk back to BeyondCorp and
inform other access decisions.

00:38:18.040 --> 00:38:22.920
So that was for
third-party apps.

00:38:22.920 --> 00:38:24.730
But we have a lot
of applications

00:38:24.730 --> 00:38:26.590
that we've written internally.

00:38:26.590 --> 00:38:31.180
And they're primarily
behind a tool

00:38:31.180 --> 00:38:40.000
called Internal Auth, which
is an identity-aware proxy.

00:38:40.000 --> 00:38:45.540
Long before Zero Trust and
BeyondCorp and all these words,

00:38:45.540 --> 00:38:48.820
Airbnb built its own
authenticated proxy for this,

00:38:48.820 --> 00:38:52.390
because we were building all
these internal applications,

00:38:52.390 --> 00:38:56.440
but we didn't want to
rebuild authentication

00:38:56.440 --> 00:38:58.910
every single time.

00:38:58.910 --> 00:39:05.020
So this is in front of tools
that are Hadoop front ends

00:39:05.020 --> 00:39:07.840
and other critical business
applications, like the launch

00:39:07.840 --> 00:39:09.310
menu.

00:39:09.310 --> 00:39:12.280
So this is what
Internal Auth has looked

00:39:12.280 --> 00:39:15.100
like for a very long time.

00:39:15.100 --> 00:39:18.210
Users come in through
VPN or 802.1X.

00:39:18.210 --> 00:39:20.080
They come through
the Direct Connect.

00:39:20.080 --> 00:39:22.990
And then they talk
to an Nginx proxy,

00:39:22.990 --> 00:39:28.060
which has some middleware on it,
which authenticates to OpenLDAP

00:39:28.060 --> 00:39:30.190
and also does MFA.

00:39:30.190 --> 00:39:32.800
So when it authenticates
to OpenLDAP,

00:39:32.800 --> 00:39:36.190
it gets that user's
information, which

00:39:36.190 --> 00:39:38.200
includes their LDAP
UID and the groups

00:39:38.200 --> 00:39:39.340
that they're a member of.

00:39:39.340 --> 00:39:41.710
And adds those to headers,
which get passed on

00:39:41.710 --> 00:39:47.350
to other applications, which
are running in EC2, which know

00:39:47.350 --> 00:39:49.430
what to do with those headers.

00:39:49.430 --> 00:39:56.170
So we wanted to rebuild this
and get the authentication

00:39:56.170 --> 00:39:59.080
to not be LDAP-based.

00:39:59.080 --> 00:40:01.900
We're moving towards
this new buzzword

00:40:01.900 --> 00:40:04.493
of password-less future.

00:40:04.493 --> 00:40:06.410
But this was still using
traditional LDAP Auth

00:40:06.410 --> 00:40:11.130
so we're thinking like,
could we add this to SAML,

00:40:11.130 --> 00:40:12.370
or add SAML auth to this.

00:40:12.370 --> 00:40:13.900
But that was kind
of complicated.

00:40:13.900 --> 00:40:20.500
So as we deployed IAP in
front of third party apps,

00:40:20.500 --> 00:40:22.750
we kept running into
the HTTP request

00:40:22.750 --> 00:40:25.990
headers, which come through
in every single request.

00:40:25.990 --> 00:40:31.040
And in them, you'll see
this goog-iap-jwt-assertion,

00:40:31.040 --> 00:40:32.350
which is this big long header.

00:40:32.350 --> 00:40:34.360
There must be a lot
of data in there.

00:40:34.360 --> 00:40:36.630
And what it is is
an assigned header,

00:40:36.630 --> 00:40:38.630
which is telling you more
about what's going on.

00:40:38.630 --> 00:40:41.440
So if we verify that
and decode that,

00:40:41.440 --> 00:40:45.155
we can see that this is
the information contained

00:40:45.155 --> 00:40:45.780
within the JWT.

00:40:49.042 --> 00:40:52.060
We get the user's email.

00:40:52.060 --> 00:40:55.660
We get their access levels,
which is really nice.

00:40:55.660 --> 00:40:57.560
Those can be reused
in the future.

00:40:57.560 --> 00:41:01.900
So if you want to have
your end applications maybe

00:41:01.900 --> 00:41:05.470
make access decisions to
different types of data instead

00:41:05.470 --> 00:41:06.980
of just access the application.

00:41:06.980 --> 00:41:08.710
If you want to have
different regions be

00:41:08.710 --> 00:41:11.570
able to access different
things, you can reuse this data.

00:41:11.570 --> 00:41:14.660
But what we were really
looking at here was the email.

00:41:14.660 --> 00:41:17.410
So this has my email in
it, but that's not really

00:41:17.410 --> 00:41:18.910
what we needed.

00:41:18.910 --> 00:41:22.330
What we needed was the
LDAP UID and the groups.

00:41:22.330 --> 00:41:25.390
We wanted to deploy this
without making any changes

00:41:25.390 --> 00:41:27.050
to the applications.

00:41:27.050 --> 00:41:30.070
So this is what
we ended up doing.

00:41:30.070 --> 00:41:34.270
This is what we've been working
on over the last few weeks

00:41:34.270 --> 00:41:35.920
actually.

00:41:35.920 --> 00:41:38.042
So this is a little bit
different from last time.

00:41:38.042 --> 00:41:39.250
It's a little bit simplified.

00:41:39.250 --> 00:41:44.200
I'm not including the full
access context manager path.

00:41:44.200 --> 00:41:47.890
But we can see here
that IAP adds this HTTP

00:41:47.890 --> 00:41:49.630
header to the requests.

00:41:49.630 --> 00:41:54.890
And now, since the user is
already logged in through IAP,

00:41:54.890 --> 00:41:57.310
our middleware has
been much simplified.

00:41:57.310 --> 00:42:01.360
So instead of the middleware
authenticating the user,

00:42:01.360 --> 00:42:07.120
it's just going to
OpenLDAP to take that email

00:42:07.120 --> 00:42:11.080
and resolve it to an LDAP UID
and get the same user and group

00:42:11.080 --> 00:42:14.980
information that we always
had to pass it along

00:42:14.980 --> 00:42:16.490
to those applications.

00:42:16.490 --> 00:42:22.060
So we still have a very similar
deployment as we've had.

00:42:22.060 --> 00:42:24.370
But users get a much
simplified experience,

00:42:24.370 --> 00:42:25.787
because they're
already logged in.

00:42:25.787 --> 00:42:28.820
And they're not having
to log in a second time.

00:42:28.820 --> 00:42:30.040
Our MFA is simplified.

00:42:30.040 --> 00:42:39.830
And we're able to bypass VPN and
802.1X for these applications.

00:42:39.830 --> 00:42:45.550
So to wrap up my
part, I have to say

00:42:45.550 --> 00:42:49.810
that not everything
is perfect with IAP

00:42:49.810 --> 00:42:51.530
and context-aware right now.

00:42:51.530 --> 00:42:53.523
So this is a pleading face.

00:42:53.523 --> 00:42:54.940
So this is everything
that I plead

00:42:54.940 --> 00:42:57.610
to Ameet about that
I've been promised

00:42:57.610 --> 00:42:59.440
is going to be
fixed in the future.

00:42:59.440 --> 00:43:04.420
So non-GCP backend support,
the way we're doing with Nginx,

00:43:04.420 --> 00:43:05.590
is kind of a hack.

00:43:05.590 --> 00:43:08.320
I mean, Nginx is a known
beast, but I don't really

00:43:08.320 --> 00:43:10.040
want to run those anymore.

00:43:10.040 --> 00:43:16.465
So in the future,
GCP load balancers

00:43:16.465 --> 00:43:18.340
are going to support
network endpoint groups.

00:43:18.340 --> 00:43:20.350
So they support network
endpoint groups--

00:43:20.350 --> 00:43:23.110
I think it was in
beta as of recently.

00:43:23.110 --> 00:43:24.147
It might be GA now.

00:43:24.147 --> 00:43:26.230
But what we really need
is network endpoint groups

00:43:26.230 --> 00:43:31.457
to be hybrid so we can
target things outside of GCP.

00:43:31.457 --> 00:43:33.290
And that would allow
us to get rid of Nginx.

00:43:33.290 --> 00:43:37.320
It would make deploying
this way easier.

00:43:37.320 --> 00:43:38.880
This is a big sticking point.

00:43:38.880 --> 00:43:42.457
Chrome is the only
supported browser for--

00:43:42.457 --> 00:43:44.290
Chrome is the only
supported desktop browser

00:43:44.290 --> 00:43:45.840
for Endpoint Verification.

00:43:45.840 --> 00:43:51.090
So if you have users using
Safari, or Firefox, or Edge,

00:43:51.090 --> 00:43:53.760
and you want to verify any
of their endpoint states,

00:43:53.760 --> 00:43:55.180
it's just not going to work.

00:43:55.180 --> 00:43:56.805
They're not going to
have access today.

00:43:58.950 --> 00:44:01.800
iOS and Android support,
not yet generally available.

00:44:01.800 --> 00:44:08.100
But I've been told
it will be beta soon.

00:44:08.100 --> 00:44:12.150
For third party apps,
because the session time

00:44:12.150 --> 00:44:15.270
of IAP sessions
is only one hour,

00:44:15.270 --> 00:44:19.400
if you're using AJAX
apps, the callbacks

00:44:19.400 --> 00:44:21.390
will start to fail
after an hour.

00:44:21.390 --> 00:44:25.980
If you write your own apps,
you can do session refreshing

00:44:25.980 --> 00:44:26.790
yourself.

00:44:26.790 --> 00:44:29.760
But if you're using
third party apps,

00:44:29.760 --> 00:44:34.560
you're probably going to
need to inject an iframe

00:44:34.560 --> 00:44:36.000
into those applications.

00:44:36.000 --> 00:44:37.470
Nginx is really good at that.

00:44:37.470 --> 00:44:41.160
That's what we're doing,
but that's a headache

00:44:41.160 --> 00:44:43.720
that you'll probably run into.

00:44:43.720 --> 00:44:47.190
And then applications that might
have a mix of thick clients

00:44:47.190 --> 00:44:49.620
and web clients that might
hit the same endpoints

00:44:49.620 --> 00:44:52.050
are going to be really
hard to deal with.

00:44:52.050 --> 00:44:54.000
But not everything is terrible.

00:44:54.000 --> 00:44:57.300
I want to leave you with
some things that I do like.

00:44:57.300 --> 00:44:59.897
So oh, they're all
appearing at once.

00:44:59.897 --> 00:45:09.870
But if a web application
can live behind a web--

00:45:09.870 --> 00:45:10.370
oh, no.

00:45:13.340 --> 00:45:17.165
There is a missing
smiley face there.

00:45:17.165 --> 00:45:19.040
If a web app can live
behind a load balancer,

00:45:19.040 --> 00:45:23.240
it's probably going to be
really easy to use IAP with it.

00:45:23.240 --> 00:45:26.840
And the Google Front
End really helps us

00:45:26.840 --> 00:45:30.890
with having performant
access to these apps.

00:45:30.890 --> 00:45:33.560
The session initiation
is really fast.

00:45:33.560 --> 00:45:37.400
And the premium
network is definitely

00:45:37.400 --> 00:45:39.480
better than the
general internet.

00:45:39.480 --> 00:45:42.170
So when we have users
coming from very far,

00:45:42.170 --> 00:45:45.980
it helps us a lot.

00:45:45.980 --> 00:45:53.750
And then the information that we
get in the JWTs is very useful.

00:45:53.750 --> 00:45:57.740
And I'm very excited about the
future state of context-aware

00:45:57.740 --> 00:46:01.700
for IAP, G Suite, and SAML
so we have consistent access

00:46:01.700 --> 00:46:02.493
to everything.

00:46:02.493 --> 00:46:03.410
AMEET JANI: Thank you.

00:46:03.410 --> 00:46:04.010
[APPLAUSE]

00:46:04.010 --> 00:46:07.360
[MUSIC PLAYING]

